1
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:07,140
A thousand years ago, at the eastern gate of
Mesopotamia, a Kurdish dynasty rose to power.

2
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:11,380
The great road from Baghdad to Khorasan
ran straight through their lands.

3
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:13,780
For nearly one hundred and thirty
years, they held this gate.

4
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:16,740
They built palaces, led cavalry armies,

5
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:20,060
and lived through alliances and
wars with neighboring dynasties.

6
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:22,060
Their name was the Annazids.

7
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,793
Welcome to Dirok.

8
00:00:23,833 --> 00:00:27,820
So you never miss the pages of Kurdish
history that others tried to erase,

9
00:00:27,860 --> 00:00:29,200
remember to subscribe.

10
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:44,660
East of the Tigris River, the plain
of Mesopotamia meets the mountains.

11
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,700
Right on this threshold
rises the city of Hulwan.

12
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:50,500
Known today as Sarpol-e Zahab, this city was,

13
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,407
according to medieval geographers,
the last city of the plain of Iraq,

14
00:00:53,447 --> 00:00:55,833
that is, of Mesopotamia.

15
00:00:55,873 --> 00:00:58,660
The Alwand stream, rising at
Hulwan, flows into the Diyala River,

16
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:00,460
and from there into the Tigris.

17
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,500
This land carries a very ancient memory.

18
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:06,420
Four thousand years ago,

19
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:08,873
the Lullubi king Anubanini had his
victory carved into the rocks of Hulwan.

20
00:01:08,913 --> 00:01:12,687
Later, the Kassites came out of this
region and took the throne of Babylon.

21
00:01:12,727 --> 00:01:17,300
For thousands of years, the ancestors of the
Kurds lived at this gate between Mesopotamia

22
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:18,600
and the mountains.

23
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:27,240
The story of the Annazids
begins around the year 990.

24
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:31,000
Abu al-Fath Muhammad ibn Annaz,
of the Kurdish Shadhanjan tribe,

25
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:33,067
seized power in the city of Hulwan.

26
00:01:33,107 --> 00:01:36,120
The dynasty took its name
from his father, Annaz.

27
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:41,040
The Shadhanjan were a powerful Kurdish community
living between the Mesopotamian plain and

28
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:41,960
the mountains.

29
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,720
With the horsemen of his tribe, Abu
al-Fath turned the balance of the region in

30
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:49,147
his favor and ruled Hulwan for twenty years.

31
00:01:49,187 --> 00:01:53,700
And so the eastern gate of Mesopotamia
passed into the hands of a Kurdish emirate.

32
00:01:58,420 --> 00:02:00,620
Hulwan was no ordinary city.

33
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:03,980
The great road linking Baghdad
to Hamadan and Khorasan passed

34
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:06,100
through here and climbed into the mountains.

35
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:09,340
Caravans laden with silk,
spices, horses and cloth,

36
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:11,420
pilgrim convoys from Khorasan,

37
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:14,540
envoys and armies all had
to pass through this gate.

38
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:18,247
The Annazids guarded the road, and
in return they collected a toll.

39
00:02:18,287 --> 00:02:21,220
But the dynasty's wealth did
not come from the road alone.

40
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:24,860
The well-watered lands of the Diyala
plain yielded wheat and barley.

41
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:28,020
The tribes spent the winter in
the mild plains of Mesopotamia,

42
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:30,740
and the summer in the cool mountain pastures.

43
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:32,460
This cycle of summer and

44
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:36,160
winter pastures made the plain and
the mountain one single homeland.

45
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:44,040
Annazid rule rested on a tribal order.

46
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:49,520
The emir was the head of the
dynasty, but he drew his power from
the horsemen of the Shadhanjan tribe.

47
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:52,520
The lands were shared among
members of the dynasty.

48
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,960
The emir's brother Muhalhil governed Shahrazur,

49
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,400
while another brother,
Surkhab, governed Bandanijin.

50
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,280
At court, envoys were received,

51
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:03,840
taxes were collected, and councils
were held with the tribal chiefs.

52
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:07,280
Important decisions were made with
the approval of the tribal elders.

53
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:11,080
This order reflected the structure of
a Kurdish tribal tradition that endured

54
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:12,380
for centuries.

55
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:19,780
The heart of Annazid power was its cavalry.

56
00:03:19,820 --> 00:03:23,060
Lightly armored Kurdish riders,
armed with lances and bows,

57
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:24,660
moved

58
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,860
swiftly across the plains and laid
ambushes in the mountain passes.

59
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:31,780
The dynasty's most precious assets were
the castles built on steep mountain slopes.

60
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:36,380
Castles such as Sirwan were both the place
where the treasury was kept and the family's

61
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:37,740
refuge in times of danger.

62
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:42,300
An emir defeated on the plain could withdraw to
his mountain castle and gather his strength again.

63
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:46,760
That is why, even when they lost the plain,
the Annazids could not easily be destroyed.

64
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:55,080
The Annazids' most powerful
neighbor was also a Kurdish dynasty:

65
00:03:55,120 --> 00:03:58,240
the Hasanwayhids, who ruled
Dinawar and its surroundings.

66
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:00,960
The two dynasties were bound by marriage ties.

67
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,280
Yet at the same time, they were fierce rivals.

68
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:07,840
Again and again, the two Kurdish families
clashed over caravan routes and mountain

69
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:08,600
passes.

70
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:12,640
When the Hasanwayhid emir Badr
ibn Hasanwayh died in 1014,

71
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,560
Hasanwayhid power collapsed.

72
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:18,260
A large part of their lands
passed to the Annazids.

73
00:04:22,980 --> 00:04:26,753
The dynasty's golden age
came with Abu al-Shawk Faris.

74
00:04:26,793 --> 00:04:30,980
Bearing the title Husam al-Dawla,
this emir ruled for thirty-five years.

75
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:34,780
Under his rule, Annazid lands
stretched from Hulwan to Kermanshah,

76
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:38,980
from Dinawar to Shahrazur,
and from Daquq to Bandanijin.

77
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:43,500
Most of these lands lay along the
eastern edge of the Mesopotamian plain.

78
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:47,340
In the balance between Baghdad and Hamadan,

79
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:50,480
the Annazid cavalry was a force
everyone had to reckon with.

80
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:59,240
The Annazids struck a skillful
balance between powerful neighbors.

81
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:02,253
To the west stood the
Abbasid caliphate in Baghdad.

82
00:05:02,293 --> 00:05:04,800
To the northwest, the Uqaylids ruled Mosul,

83
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:07,840
and to the southwest, the
Mazyadids ruled at Hilla.

84
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:13,600
To the east, the Kakuyids of Isfahan
laid claim to Hamadan and Dinawar.

85
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:18,680
Abu al-Shawk sometimes allied with these
neighbors, and sometimes fought them.

86
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:23,520
Envoys, gifts and marriage ties were
weapons as important as the sword.

87
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:27,920
Farther away, the Kurdish dynasties of the
Marwanids and the Shaddadids were also on the rise

88
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:29,260
in the same age.

89
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:36,940
But a wound from within weakened the dynasty.

90
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:40,300
Struggles for the throne broke
out between brothers and nephews.

91
00:05:40,340 --> 00:05:44,940
The rivalry between Abu al-Shawk and his
brother Muhalhil split the tribes in two.

92
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:49,900
Later, Abu al-Shawk's son Sadi sought
outside help against his uncle Muhalhil.

93
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:53,720
This division opened the way for
a new storm coming from the east.

94
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:03,320
In the middle of the eleventh century, the
Seljuks swiftly conquered the Iranian plateau.

95
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:07,320
In 1046, the Seljuk commander
Ibrahim Inal marched on Hulwan.

96
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:09,320
The city was besieged, and it fell.

97
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,720
In the same year, Abu al-Shawk died
in the mountain fortress of Sirwan.

98
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:17,160
The Annazids lost their cities
on the Mesopotamian plain,

99
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:19,300
but they did not give up the mountains.

100
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:28,513
The dynasty survived for seventy more
years in its castles and in the mountains.

101
00:06:28,553 --> 00:06:34,700
Muhalhil and the emirs descended from him
ruled around Shahrazur, Daquq and Sirwan.

102
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:39,220
Around the year 1117, the Annazid
dynasty left the stage of history.

103
00:06:39,260 --> 00:06:43,740
This was the story of one of the last
great Kurdish dynasties to rule at the

104
00:06:43,780 --> 00:06:45,520
eastern gate of Mesopotamia.

105
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:52,120
The historian Vladimir Minorsky called this age,

106
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:56,600
when Daylamite and Kurdish
dynasties rose to power,

107
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:57,360
the Iranian Intermezzo.

108
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:01,760
Together with the Marwanids, the
Shaddadids and the Hasanwayhids,

109
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:05,720
the Annazids were a strong link in the political
presence of the Kurds in the Middle Ages.

110
00:07:05,760 --> 00:07:10,160
Today, the Kurds living around Sarpol-e Zahab,

111
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:13,020
Shahrazur and Daquq are the heirs
of this thousand-year-old story.

112
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:17,580
One tribe, one pass, an emirate
of one hundred and thirty years.

113
00:07:17,620 --> 00:07:21,740
The pages of Kurdish history that
others tried to erase, here on Dirok.

114
00:07:21,780 --> 00:07:26,060
If you enjoyed this video, don't forget
to subscribe and share your thoughts in

115
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:27,120
the comments.
